## Overview
#c1[overview,i5] The HCS system requires Node.js 18.17 or higher and provides zero runtime dependencies.
  > README.md::Install #c24666a9bbdc
#c2[overview,i5] Anchors identify file paths, optional line ranges or symbols, and truncated SHA-256 hashes to track source content stability.
  > src/anchors.ts:44 #33a598e61fc8
  > src/anchors.ts::hashText #bc4793117b78
  > src/format.ts::Anchor #a1011c696164
#c3[overview,i5] The CLI supports commands for checking anchors, sealing updates, compiling sources via LLMs, scoring context fidelity, and running an MCP server.
  > src/cli.ts:28 #841032f6e7f2
  > src/cli.ts:30-31 #64a7418943da
  > src/cli.ts:43-44 #07e90b284af3
  > src/cli.ts:48 #c2fe074401d9
  > src/cli.ts:37 #8af5d5a61295
#c4[overview,i5] Security guards scan sources and claims for injection-like patterns and high-risk content, excluding suspect items from renders.
  > src/cli.ts::printSourceFindings #e918cff42a51
  > src/guard.ts:7-9 #8cde311423ee
  > src/guard.ts::scanSource #4653fed397c1
#c5[overview,i5] The HCS format supports multiple versions with specific magic lines, parsing and serializing structured elements like claims, probes, and anchors.
  > src/format.ts:6 #214270a35d40
  > src/format.ts:111-115 #c93d6425ac7c
  > src/format.ts:153-161 #317019fb98b5
  > src/format.ts::serialize #ae589dcd81a8
#c6[overview,i5] LLM interactions include usage tracking, retry logic with exponential backoff for failed requests, and JSON extraction with fallbacks.
  > src/llm.ts::createLLM #e1653e3a53e5
  > src/llm.ts::postJSON #d1a90aeac977
  > src/llm.ts::extractJSON #10d075d4cadb
#c7[overview,i5] Source collection and symbol processing handle file parsing, language detection, chunking, and enforcing root-directory access restrictions.
  > src/anchors.ts:58-59 #45d32991606e
  > src/sources.ts::chunkFile #ffc6242f4452
  > src/symbols.ts::languageOf #b03b3b4eb531
  > src/symbols.ts::listSymbols #20db3769bf48
#c8[overview,i5] Context rendering filters and ranks claims based on verification status, security risk, and token budgets to generate XML-like representations.
  > src/render.ts::selectClaims #c510bd10896d
  > src/render.ts::renderContext #31ee6bf0bfa6

## (root)
#c9[i4] HCS requires Node.js version 18.17 or higher and has zero runtime dependencies.
  > README.md::Install #c24666a9bbdc
#c10[i3] The 'manual' tag identifies human-written claims that tools must not automatically delete and which have a default importance of 5.
  > SPEC.md::"Standard tags" #18bde28f30c7

## src
#c11[i5] The SourceCache class restricts file access to the project root and refuses paths that escape it via symlinks or relative segments.
  > src/anchors.ts:58-59 #45d32991606e
#c12[i4] Symbol anchors are hashed with all whitespace removed to ensure stability against reformatting and re-indenting.
  > src/anchors.ts:44 #33a598e61fc8
#c13[i3] The normalizeLines function standardizes text by converting CRLF to LF, removing trailing whitespace from lines, and stripping trailing empty lines.
  > src/anchors.ts::normalizeLines #1f9f0385cd31
#c14[i4] The findWindow function searches for a matching hash by expanding outward from the original line position to find the nearest relocation.
  > src/anchors.ts:176 #689eb51d4794
#c15[i5] The seal function updates anchors by applying new hashes to unsealed items, following moved symbols or ranges, and optionally rehashing stale content.
  > src/anchors.ts:215-217 #93b98d29f8e4
#c16[i3] Anchor hashes are derived from the first 12 characters of a SHA-256 digest.
  > src/anchors.ts::hashText #bc4793117b78
#c17[i4] The findMovedSymbol function attempts to locate a symbol in other files of the same language by matching its content hash and name suffix.
  > src/anchors.ts:122 #85c181e4af28
#c18[i3] Anchor status severity is ranked from 'ok' (lowest) to 'missing' (highest) to determine the overall status of a claim or probe.
  > src/anchors.ts::SEVERITY #b0541f5bc144
#c19[i4] The PathReference interface tracks a file path's location, existence status, and whether it is a 'bare' filename without directory separators.
  > src/audit.ts::PathReference #84a49e01386c
#c20[i3] The looksLikePath function filters out strings that are too short (under 3 chars), too long (over 200 chars), or contain whitespace and shell-sensitive characters.
  > src/audit.ts::looksLikePath #f26ac1eac79d
#c21[i2] Strings starting with '@' or '-' are excluded from being identified as file paths.
  > src/audit.ts::looksLikePath #f26ac1eac79d
#c22[i4] A string is only considered a path if it contains a directory separator or a file extension, and does not look like a version number or code identifier.
  > src/audit.ts::looksLikePath #f26ac1eac79d
#c23[i3] Dotted identifiers like 'obj.method' are rejected unless they use a recognized file extension like .md, .ts, or .json.
  > src/audit.ts::looksLikePath #f26ac1eac79d
#c24[i5] findPathReferences extracts candidates from backticks and Markdown links, stripping line numbers and anchors before validation.
  > src/audit.ts::findPathReferences #062b34fc2880
#c25[i5] The module uses existsSync and resolve to verify if extracted paths exist relative to a provided root directory.
  > src/audit.ts::findPathReferences #062b34fc2880
#c26[i3] The findPathReferences function maintains a 'seen' Set to ensure each unique path is only reported once.
  > src/audit.ts::findPathReferences #062b34fc2880
#c27[i4] The `hcs check` command verifies anchors against sources and exits with code 1 if items are stale, missing, or (in strict mode) unsealed or suspect.
  > src/cli.ts:28 #841032f6e7f2
#c28[i4] The `hcs seal` command updates missing hashes and follows moved anchors; using `--rehash` accepts current content for stale anchors and clears existing certificates.
  > src/cli.ts:30-31 #64a7418943da
#c29[i5] Claims containing injection-like text or tagged as 'suspect' are excluded from rendering to prevent prompt injection.
  > src/cli.ts::printSourceFindings #e918cff42a51
#c30[i5] The `hcs compile` command generates an HCS file from source paths using an LLM, supporting incremental updates and probe quality filtering via `--filter-known`.
  > src/cli.ts:43-44 #07e90b284af3
#c31[i4] The `hcs score` command evaluates context fidelity using an answerer LLM and optional judge LLMs, producing a certificate that is invalidated if the context changes.
  > src/cli.ts:48 #c2fe074401d9
#c32[i4] The `hcs serve` command implements an MCP server over stdio, enforcing that source reads remain within the specified root directory for security.
  > src/cli.ts:37 #8af5d5a61295
#c33[i3] The CLI automatically reruns itself with the `--use-env-proxy` flag if `HTTPS_PROXY` is detected and the Node.js version supports it.
  > src/cli.ts:555 #c6b6e0b00420
#c34[i4] The compiler splits source files into chunks of a configurable size, defaulting to 250 lines, for processing by an LLM.
  > src/compile.ts:242 #0f6a238c9a69
#c35[i5] Claims are anchored to specific symbols if anchorMode is 'symbols' and a matching symbol is found; otherwise, they default to line ranges.
  > src/compile.ts::anchorFor #5e3194b4874f
#c36[i5] The compiler supports incremental mode, preserving verified claims and probes from a previous document while only re-compiling changed or new files.
  > src/compile.ts:241 #25d868a268e9
#c37[i4] If an LLM quota is exhausted mid-run, the compiler records unfinished chunks in the '@incomplete' header to allow later resumption.
  > src/compile.ts:302-304 #cdedf6e0ca93
#c38[i5] Claims and probes are discarded if their required 'quote' field cannot be exactly located within the source chunk.
  > src/compile.ts::anchorFor #5e3194b4874f
#c39[i3] The synthesize function generates up to 8 overview claims that describe architecture and cross-cutting rules by combining existing verified claims.
  > src/compile.ts::synthesize #887d6e9530d9
#c40[i3] Manual claims are never automatically dropped during incremental compilation, even if the files they reference are recompiled.
  > src/compile.ts:280 #d06e74f7627a
#c41[i3] The compiler limits the maximum length of an anchor range to 40 lines to prevent anchors from going stale due to unrelated edits.
  > src/compile.ts::MAX_ANCHOR_LINES #795821f22731
#c42[i4] The HCS format supports versions 0.1, 0.2, and 0.3, with 0.3 introducing probe tags and 0.2 adding path::Symbol anchors.
  > src/format.ts:6 #214270a35d40
#c43[i5] An Anchor identifies a file path, an optional symbol or 1-based line range, and an optional truncated SHA-256 hash.
  > src/format.ts::Anchor #a1011c696164
#c44[i5] The parse function enforces a magic line matching HCS/0.1, HCS/0.2, or HCS/0.3 at the start of the document.
